    "result_text": "Done. The full test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe `sharesHousehold` **allow** logic already existed in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` (it lets an owner through when another owner shares the same last name and address). The only missing piece was assigning and returning a shared `householdId`.\n\nI implemented it as a **derived, deterministic value** rather than persisted state, which kept structural impact minimal \u2014 no controller, model, or DB/schema/data changes:\n\n1. **`HouseholdMatcher.householdId(Owner)`** (new small static method in a tiny class) \u2014 derives a stable id from the *same* normalized last-name + address that `sameHousehold` already uses, via `UUID.nameUUIDFromBytes`. Because it depends only on the shared household attributes, any two same-household owners produce the identical id with no coordination \u2014 so \"assign both the same householdId\" is satisfied by construction.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.toOwnerDto`** \u2014 one `@Mapping(... expression ...)` line, exactly like the existing `displayName`/`initials` expressions, populates the DTO field.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `householdId` as a read-only string on the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n\nWhy this keeps the cost low: I touched only 3 files and added behaviour as small cohesive units in low-complexity classes (`HouseholdMatcher`, the mapper) instead of growing the heavy `addOwner` method or its large controller class. Every function changed is trivial (CC 1). No new DB column was needed, which also avoided editing the positional `INSERT`s in `data.sql`.\n\nThe new `coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` test passes (owners `b` and `c`, sharing `a`'s last name and address, return the same non-blank `householdId`), and the rest of the suite stays green.",
